[38;5;252m[1m## The interesting part: four defects, all in the verification[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Mutation testing ([38;5;10mscripts/mutation_check.sh[0m, 12 injected defects) is what exposed these — passing tests alone would have hidden all four:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1mPhases B and C were blind to [38;5;10mdriver[0m branch inversion and to [38;5;10mbad[0m forwarding to [38;5;10mgood[0m.[22m C's [38;5;10mbad()[0m and [38;5;10mgood()[0m print [3midentical[23m bytes ([38;5;10m"0\n"[0m — both copy [38;5;10msource[10] = {0}[0m and print [38;5;10mdata[0][0m), so no stdout differential can see which branch [38;5;10mdriver[0m took. Mutants [38;5;10mdriver_inverted[0m and [38;5;10mfold_bad_into_good[0m left the suite green. Runtime interposition isn't usable here: the C [38;5;10m.so[0m calls [38;5;10mbad[0m/[38;5;10mgood[0m through the PLT while Rust reaches them via its own GOT, so interposing would manufacture a difference that isn't real. I closed it in [38;5;10mtests/structural.rs[0m by resolving branch direction and the call graph from [38;5;10mobjdump -d[0m plus the [38;5;10mobjdump -R[0m relocation table — works for direct PLT calls (C) and GOT-indirect calls and tail-calls (Rust release). Both mutants now die.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [1mA brittle structural test produced a false kill.[22m My first "printLine has a null guard" check passed or failed depending on which profile's [38;5;10m.so[0m was newest — in a dev build Rust lowers the check to an out-of-line [38;5;10mis_null[0m call, so no [38;5;10mcmp $0x0[0m appears in the body. Deleted: the guard is fully observable ([38;5;10merr_01[0m kills the drop-the-guard mutant), so the check added brittleness and no coverage.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
3. [1m[38;5;10m.so[0m selection was nondeterministic[22m ("newest of debug/release"). Pinned to dev-profile, [38;5;10mDRIVER_RUST_SO[0m override, both profiles run explicitly.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
4. [1m[38;5;10mverify_all.sh[0m reported "ok" for a run that executed zero tests[22m — it counted with [38;5;10mgrep -c '^test .* ok$'[0m under [38;5;10mcargo test -q[0m, which prints no per-test lines. Counts now come from [38;5;10mtest result: ok. N passed[0m and are asserted against the [38;5;10m#[test][0m count.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two mutants survive [1mby design[22m and are declared as such: [38;5;10mbad_prints_index_1[0m and [38;5;10mloop_off_by_one[0m. Since [38;5;10msource[0m is all zeros and only [38;5;10mdata[0][0m is printed, every index and every loop bound ≥ 1 prints [38;5;10m0[0m — no consumer of the [3moriginal C[23m can distinguish them either, so there is nothing for a differential test to observe. I verified those by reading the Rust against the C directly, and recorded them in [38;5;10mCONFIGS.md[0m rather than faking coverage.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The [38;5;10mbad()[0m defect is preserved, not repaired: [38;5;10malloca(10)[0m for 40 bytes of writes. [38;5;10merr_10[0m hammers it 512× interleaved with the other entry points to confirm the overrun stays unobservable on both sides.[0m[0m
